    I had a similar case once .... where i was extremely interesting on=20
squid delay_pool functionality but didnt want to waste CPU and I/O=20
because of squid.

    I then installed squid with a NULL cache_dir, which means no cache=20
at all being done. On the very few days i got logging just to check=20
everything was OK. Some days after, i disabled logging as well.

    http_access ACLs were pretty simple so i dont think they would waste =

too much cpu power.

    Maybe disabling cache and logging you can use squid to acchieve your =

bandwidth limitation with some GREAT and FLEXIBLE ACLs, which i dont=20
think you'll be able to acchieve with others solutions .....  and=20
because of disabled features, maybe your CPU and I/O overhead wouldnt be =

that big .....


Umut Arus escreveu:
> Thank for reply. But squid can not handle this type of limitations and =

> waste excessive cpu. We removed that configuration before.

